course: dictionary
lesson: 14280
title: Making things normal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Normalise is a verb. It comes from the word normal, which means ordinary or usual. The clues in the sentence show which meaning fits. «We want to normalise asking for help in our class.» The words want and asking for help tell you that the author wants asking for help to feel ordinary, something everyone does without fuss.
explain: «After the storm, the buses slowly normalised and ran on time again.» Here the buses went back to their usual way of running. The word again is the clue, so normalise can mean return to a normal state.
explain: «Scientists normalise the data so the results can be compared.» This is a different meaning. The numbers are changed onto a standard scale, so they can be compared fairly. To check any meaning, swap your idea into the sentence. Does "make the data ordinary" or "put the data on a standard scale" fit best?
fact: Normalise is a verb, so it changes: normalise, normalises, normalised, normalising.
fact: «Normalise» can mean to make something seem ordinary, so people accept it.
fact: «Normalise» can mean to return something to a normal state, as in «relations normalised».
fact: In maths and science, to normalise means to change numbers to a standard scale so they can be compared.
fact: «Don't normalise bullying» means don't let bullying seem ordinary or acceptable.
exercise: «We want to normalise asking for help.» What does normalise mean here? | *To make asking for help seem ordinary and fine | To shout loudly about help | To stop people asking for help | To forget how to ask
exercise: «After the storm, the buses slowly normalised.» What does normalised mean here? | Stopped for ever | *Went back to their usual way of running | Got much bigger | Were painted new colours
exercise: Scientists normalise numbers so they can be compared. Why do they do this? | To make the numbers bigger | To add a colour to each number | To hide the numbers from others | *To put them on a standard scale so they can be fairly compared
test: Yesterday the teacher ___ the scores so that every class could be compared. Which word fits? | normalising | normalise | *normalised | normalises
test: «Don't normalise bullying.» What does this mean? | Bullying is a fun sport | Make bullying stop by shouting | Tell the bullies to be happy | *Don't let bullying seem ordinary or acceptable
exam: «The two countries want to normalise relations after the long war.» What does normalise mean here? | *Return their relations to a normal, friendly state | Count how many countries there are | Break off all talks for ever | Make the war seem bigger
